WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.400
Watch the stars orbit the same dark center.

00:00:02.460 --> 00:00:04.920
Their paths and speeds reveal the mass pulling

00:00:04.920 --> 00:00:07.940
on them. Astronomers combine changes in position with

00:00:07.940 --> 00:00:11.040
Doppler measurements of radial velocity. The result is

00:00:11.040 --> 00:00:14.260
about four million solar masses concentrated at Sagittarius

00:00:14.260 --> 00:00:17.040
A star. One star, S zero two, takes

00:00:17.040 --> 00:00:19.960
about fifteen years to orbit. These central stellar

00:00:19.960 --> 00:00:22.700
orbits measure the black hole. The rotation curve

00:00:22.700 --> 00:00:25.180
across the much larger Galactic disk measures how

00:00:25.180 --> 00:00:26.900
mass is distributed through the Galaxy.

00:00:27.630 --> 00:00:29.550
A star passing too close to a black

00:00:29.550 --> 00:00:32.890
hole experiences different gravitational pulls across its body.

00:00:32.930 --> 00:00:35.190
The near side is pulled more strongly than

00:00:35.190 --> 00:00:37.710
the far side. The star stretches along that

00:00:37.710 --> 00:00:40.350
direction and is torn into a stream. This

00:00:40.350 --> 00:00:43.310
is stellar spaghettification. Keep the difference in pull

00:00:43.310 --> 00:00:45.570
in view: tidal gravity changes the shape of

00:00:45.570 --> 00:00:48.010
the star. The size and timing here are

00:00:48.010 --> 00:00:48.530
schematic.

00:00:49.260 --> 00:00:51.840
Compare the motion in these two galaxies. In

00:00:51.840 --> 00:00:54.820
a spiral, stars orbit in a rotating disk.

00:00:55.000 --> 00:00:58.200
Gas and dust support continuing star formation. Young

00:00:58.200 --> 00:01:01.640
population one stars occupy the disk; older population

00:01:01.640 --> 00:01:04.920
two stars occupy the halo and bulge. An

00:01:04.920 --> 00:01:07.720
elliptical has much more random stellar motion, little

00:01:07.720 --> 00:01:11.420
rotation, and mostly old population two stars. It

00:01:11.420 --> 00:01:13.860
usually has little gas or dust and no

00:01:13.860 --> 00:01:17.500
active star formation. Hubble's tuning fork classifies these

00:01:17.500 --> 00:01:20.680
shapes. It is not an evolutionary sequence from

00:01:20.680 --> 00:01:22.040
elliptical to spiral.

00:01:22.770 --> 00:01:27.070
Galaxies interact through gravity. During an encounter, tidal

00:01:27.070 --> 00:01:30.310
forces reshape the galaxies and compress their gas.

00:01:30.450 --> 00:01:32.990
The compressed gas triggers a burst of star

00:01:32.990 --> 00:01:36.650
formation, called a starburst. In a central passage,

00:01:36.730 --> 00:01:39.030
a smaller galaxy moves through the middle of

00:01:39.030 --> 00:01:41.430
a larger one. A ripple of star formation

00:01:41.430 --> 00:01:44.710
spreads into a ring. Follow the sequence: encounter,

00:01:44.850 --> 00:01:47.950
gas compression, then new stars. A collision can

00:01:47.950 --> 00:01:50.950
change a galaxy's structure; the tuning fork itself

00:01:50.950 --> 00:01:53.410
does not describe an evolutionary path.
